Column Description

This table combines bacterial genomic association results with survival and logistic regression analyses, variant annotation, and nucleotide-frequency information.

The statistical columns are organized into four main sections:

1. Scoary association analysis
2. Cox proportional hazards regression
3. Crude (unadjusted) logistic regression
4. Adjusted logistic regression including confounders

The remaining columns provide genomic variant annotations and nucleotide frequencies.

⸻

1. Scoary association analysis

The following columns are derived from Scoary and describe the association between the presence/absence of a genomic feature and the phenotype of interest.

Column	Description
Gene	Gene, variant, or genomic feature identifier used in the association analysis.
Non.unique.Gene.name	Alternative/non-unique gene name, where available.
Annotation	Functional annotation of the genomic feature.
Number_pos_present_in	Number of phenotype-positive isolates in which the feature is present.
Number_neg_present_in	Number of phenotype-negative isolates in which the feature is present.
Number_pos_not_present_in	Number of phenotype-positive isolates in which the feature is absent.
Number_neg_not_present_in	Number of phenotype-negative isolates in which the feature is absent.
Sensitivity	Percentage of phenotype-positive isolates carrying the feature.
Specificity	Percentage of phenotype-negative isolates in which the feature is absent.
Odds_ratio	Scoary odds ratio for the association between feature presence and the phenotype.
Naive_p	Uncorrected p-value from the Scoary association test.
Bonferroni_p	Scoary p-value adjusted for multiple testing using Bonferroni correction.
Benjamini_H_p	Scoary p-value adjusted using the Benjamini–Hochberg false discovery rate (FDR) procedure.
Max_Pairwise_comparisons	Maximum number of phylogenetically informative pairwise comparisons available for the feature.
Max_supporting_pairs	Maximum number of pairwise comparisons supporting the observed association.
Max_opposing_pairs	Maximum number of pairwise comparisons opposing the observed association.
Best_pairwise_comp_p	Most significant p-value from the pairwise-comparison analysis.
Worst_pairwise_comp_p	Least significant p-value from the pairwise-comparison analysis.

The pairwise-comparison statistics provide additional information about whether an association is supported after considering the phylogenetic/population structure of the isolates.

⸻

2. Cox proportional hazards regression

These columns report results from the Cox proportional hazards model, used to assess the association between the genomic feature and the time-to-event outcome.

Column	Description
cox_coef	Cox regression coefficient (log hazard ratio) for the genomic feature.
cox_HR	Hazard ratio (HR), calculated as exp(cox_coef). HR > 1 indicates a higher event hazard, whereas HR < 1 indicates a lower event hazard, conditional on how the event was defined.
cox_SE	Standard error of the Cox regression coefficient.
cox_z	Wald z statistic for the Cox regression coefficient.
cox_p	P-value for the association between the genomic feature and the time-to-event outcome in the Cox model.

⸻

3. Crude logistic regression

These columns report results from the unadjusted (crude) logistic regression, in which the genomic feature is tested without adjustment for potential confounders.

Column	Description
crude_OR	Unadjusted odds ratio (OR) for the association between the genomic feature and the binary outcome.
crude_lower	Lower bound of the confidence interval for the crude odds ratio.
crude_upper	Upper bound of the confidence interval for the crude odds ratio.
crude_midp_p	P-value from the crude association analysis using the mid-P exact test.
crude_fisher_p	P-value from Fisher’s exact test for the crude association.
crude_chisq_p	P-value from the chi-square test for the crude association.

An OR > 1 indicates higher odds of the outcome among isolates carrying the feature, while an OR < 1 indicates lower odds, according to the coding of the outcome variable.

⸻

4. Adjusted logistic regression

These columns report results from a multivariable logistic regression model adjusted for the specified confounder(s).

Column	Description
adjusted_logit_coef	Adjusted logistic regression coefficient (log odds ratio) for the genomic feature after accounting for the included confounder(s).
adjusted_logit_SE	Standard error of the adjusted logistic regression coefficient.
adjusted_logit_z	Wald z statistic for the adjusted genomic-feature coefficient.
adjusted_logit_p	P-value for the genomic feature after adjustment for the included confounder(s).

The adjusted odds ratio can be obtained as:

adjusted OR = exp(adjusted_logit_coef)

These results should be interpreted as the association between the genomic feature and the outcome conditional on the confounder(s) included in the model.

