5. Variant annotation

The following columns describe the genomic position and predicted functional consequence of sequence variants.

Column	Description
CHROM	Chromosome, contig, or reference sequence containing the variant.
POS	Genomic position of the variant.
TYPE	Variant type, such as SNP, insertion, or deletion.
REF	Reference allele.
ALT	Alternative allele.
EVIDENCE	Evidence/supporting information associated with the variant annotation.
FTYPE	Type of genomic feature affected by the variant.
STRAND	Strand orientation of the affected genomic feature.
NT_POS	Nucleotide position of the variant within the affected feature.
AA_POS	Amino-acid position affected by the variant, where applicable.
EFFECT	Predicted functional consequence of the variant (e.g., synonymous, nonsynonymous, intergenic).
LOCUS_TAG	Locus tag of the affected gene.
GENE	Gene name associated with the variant.
PRODUCT	Annotated product/function of the affected gene.

⸻

6. Nucleotide frequencies

These columns summarize nucleotide frequencies at the corresponding genomic position across the analysed isolates.

Column	Description
missing	Proportion of isolates with missing nucleotide information at the genomic position.
A_ratio	Proportion of isolates carrying adenine (A) at the position.
C_ratio	Proportion of isolates carrying cytosine (C) at the position.
T_ratio	Proportion of isolates carrying thymine (T) at the position.
G_ratio	Proportion of isolates carrying guanine (G) at the position.

⸻

Interpretation

The different statistical analyses address related but distinct questions.

Scoary tests the association between genomic feature presence/absence and the phenotype while providing pairwise statistics intended to help account for bacterial population structure.

Cox regression evaluates whether the genomic feature is associated with the rate of occurrence of an event over follow-up time.

Crude logistic regression evaluates the unadjusted association between the genomic feature and a binary outcome.

Adjusted logistic regression evaluates the association after accounting for the specified potential confounder(s).

Therefore, p-values and effect estimates from these analyses should not be interpreted as interchangeable.

⸻

Missing values

NA indicates that a statistic or annotation was unavailable or not applicable to that genomic feature.

For variant rows, annotation fields may be NA when a variant cannot be mapped to an annotated genomic feature or when the corresponding annotation is unavailable.
